## 2D DP Explanation

The same idea can also be expressed using a 2D table.

Define:

    dp[i][j]

as the number of ways to form the first `j` characters of `t` using the first `i` characters of `s`.

If:

    s[i - 1] != t[j - 1]

we cannot use `s[i - 1]`, so:

    dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j]

If:

    s[i - 1] == t[j - 1]

we have two choices:

- Skip `s[i - 1]`
- Use `s[i - 1]`

Therefore:

    dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j] + dp[i - 1][j - 1]

The 1D solution is simply a space-optimized version of this recurrence.

---

## Correctness

For every character in `s`, there are only two possibilities when it matches the current character of `t`:

- We skip it.
- We use it to match that character of `t`.

The DP transition counts both possibilities.

When the characters do not match, the current character of `s` cannot contribute to the subsequence, so the number of ways remains unchanged.

Because `dp[0] = 1`, all valid ways of constructing prefixes of `t` are built correctly.

After processing all characters of `s`, `dp[len(t)]` contains exactly the number of distinct subsequences of `s` equal to `t`.

